  "name": "24 HR URINARY CATECHOLAMINE",
  "code": "1051",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p>This test measures the total amount of <strong data-start=\"166\" data-end=\"184\">catecholamines</strong> (dopamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine) excreted in the urine over 24 hours.</p>\n<p data-start=\"263\" data-end=\"287\">It is primarily used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"289\" data-end=\"602\">\n<li data-start=\"289\" data-end=\"427\">\n<p data-start=\"291\" data-end=\"344\">Diagnose <strong data-start=\"300\" data-end=\"334\">catecholamine-secreting tumors</strong>, such as:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"347\" data-end=\"427\">\n<li data-start=\"347\" data-end=\"369\">\n<p data-start=\"349\" data-end=\"369\"><strong data-start=\"349\" data-end=\"369\">Pheochromocytoma</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"372\" data-end=\"391\">\n<p data-start=\"374\" data-end=\"391\"><strong data-start=\"374\" data-end=\"391\">Paraganglioma</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"394\" data-end=\"427\">\n<p data-start=\"396\" data-end=\"427\"><strong data-start=\"396\" data-end=\"413\">Neuroblastoma</strong> (in children)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"428\" data-end=\"552\">\n<p data-start=\"430\" data-end=\"456\">Evaluate symptoms such as:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"459\" data-end=\"552\">\n<li data-start=\"459\" data-end=\"496\">\n<p data-start=\"461\" data-end=\"496\"><strong data-start=\"461\" data-end=\"496\">Severe or episodic hypertension</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"499\" data-end=\"514\">\n<p data-start=\"501\" data-end=\"514\"><strong data-start=\"501\" data-end=\"514\">Headaches</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"517\" data-end=\"535\">\n<p data-start=\"519\" data-end=\"535\"><strong data-start=\"519\" data-end=\"535\">Palpitations</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"538\" data-end=\"552\">\n<p data-start=\"540\" data-end=\"552\"><strong data-start=\"540\" data-end=\"552\">Sweating</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"553\" data-end=\"602\">\n<p data-start=\"555\" data-end=\"602\">Monitor treatment or recurrence of known tumors</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pheochromocytoma / Paraganglioma</strong></td>\n<td>Tumors of adrenal or extra-adrenal chromaffin cells lead to excess catecholamine secretion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Neuroblastoma</strong> (pediatric)</td>\n<td>A malignant tumor of sympathetic tissue leading to increased urinary catecholamines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stress or extreme physical activity</strong></td>\n<td>May cause temporary elevation due to sympathetic overactivity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Withdrawal from certain medications</strong></td>\n<td>E.g., <strong>clonidine withdrawal</strong> can cause increased catecholamines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hypoglycemia or severe illness</strong></td>\n<td>Stress-induced sympathetic activation can elevate levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Obstructive sleep apnea</strong></td>\n<td>Intermittent hypoxia may increase catecholamine excretion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
